# spark in nano -- your own AI, on your own machine (spark.forgewright.ai).
# nano's `include` reads only syntax files, so append these lines to your
# own ~/.nanorc:
#
#     cat ~/.config/nano/spark/spark.nanorc >> ~/.nanorc
#
#   M-S  the execute prompt opens pre-filled with `|spark edit `; type
#        your words ("shorter", "fix grammar", "? is this clear"), press
#        Enter -- the whole buffer, or the marked region (M-A marks), is
#        piped through spark and replaced by the answer; M-U undoes
#   M-F  fix spelling and punctuation, one keystroke, same pipe
#
# An ask (`? words`) answers through the same pipe: the answer replaces
# the text -- read it, then M-U brings your text back. Nothing runs until
# you press Enter; nothing leaves this machine but the piped text, to the
# brain spark is configured for -- nano's pipe carries no file name, so
# not even that. Needs GNU nano 5.4 or newer and spark 1.7 or newer.
bind M-S "{execute}|spark edit " main
bind M-F "{execute}|spark edit fix spelling and punctuation{enter}" main
